      {
       "uid": "ea89d5a5-863a-4d4f-83d7-da790ac4b14b",
       "componentName": "TitleAndParagraph",
       "dataSource": "/sitecore/content/Academy/Academy/Academy/Home/Veterinary/Canine atopic dermatitis and the owner/Data/Title and Paragraph 1",
       "restrictedPlaceHolder": null,
       "params": {
        "renderingId": "{C6E02A45-C0BD-4D75-BB75-601DC8099EB2}"
       },
       "fields": {
        "HeadingLevel": {
         "id": "b88aac1c-b17b-4af2-9278-aeaf6aa51696",
         "url": "/en/presentation/styles/heading-levels/h2",
         "name": "h2",
         "displayName": "h2",
         "fields": {
          "Value": {
           "value": "h2"
          }
         }
        },
        "Text": {
         "value": "<p>A physical check-up 2 or 3 weeks later not only enables a second clinical examination (including aural and cytological checks), it allows further discussion on treatment and logical planning for future management. This can be aided by using a quality-of-life scale [<strong title=\"Noli C, Minafo G, Galzerano M. Quality of life of dogs with skin diseases and their owners; Part 1: development and validation of a questionnaire. Vet Dermatol 2011;22(4):335-343.\" class=\"reference-snippet\">8</strong>], whereby identification of the priority factors can greatly assist in making therapeutic choices (Table 3). Information should essentially be provided in stages, with each consultation emphasizing only the necessary points for understanding the treatment.</p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<div class=\"rc-table\">\n<div class=\"rc-scroll--x\">\n<table class=\"rc-table__table\">\n    <caption style=\"text-align: left;\">Table 3. A quality-of-life survey can help prioritize the factors to be considered when dealing with a dog with CAD [<strong title=\"Noli C, Minafo G, Galzerano M. Quality of life of dogs with skin diseases and their owners; Part 1: development and validation of a questionnaire. Vet Dermatol 2011;22(4):335-343.\" class=\"reference-snippet\">8</strong>]. Scoring each factor (on a scale of 1 to 5) will identify the main criteria for both the dog and the owner.</caption>\n    <thead class=\"rc-table__thead\">\n        <tr class=\"rc-table__row\">\n            <th class=\"rc-table__th rc-espilon\">Dog factors</th>\n            <th class=\"rc-table__th rc-espilon\">Owner factors</th>\n        </tr>\n    </thead>\n    <tbody class=\"rc-table__tbody\">\n        <tr class=\"rc-table__row\">\n            <td class=\"rc-table__td\">\n            <div>&bull;<span style=\"white-space: pre;\">\t</span>Disease severity</div>\n            <div>&bull;<span style=\"white-space: pre;\">\t</span>Behavior/mood&nbsp;</div>\n            <div>&bull;<span style=\"white-space: pre;\">\t</span>Sleep</div>\n            <div>&bull;<span style=\"white-space: pre;\">\t</span>Eating behavior</div>\n            <div>&bull;<span style=\"white-space: pre;\">\t</span>Work/play</div>\n            <div>&bull;<span style=\"white-space: pre;\">\t</span>Social relations</div>\n            <div>&bull;<span style=\"white-space: pre;\">\t</span>Behavioral changes</div>\n            <div>&bull;<span style=\"white-space: pre;\">\t</span>Treatments</div>\n            </td>\n            <td class=\"rc-table__td\">\n            <div>&bull;<span style=\"white-space: pre;\">\t</span>Loss of time</div>\n            <div>&bull;<span style=\"white-space: pre;\">\t</span>Exhaustion</div>\n            <div>&bull;<span style=\"white-space: pre;\">\t</span>Family activities</div>\n            <div>&bull;<span style=\"white-space: pre;\">\t</span>Cost</div>\n            <div>&bull;<span style=\"white-space: pre;\">\t</span>Emotional distress</div>\n            <div>&bull;<span style=\"white-space: pre;\">\t</span>Physical discomfort</div>\n            <div>&bull;<span style=\"white-space: pre;\">\t</span>Family relations</div>\n            </td>\n        </tr>\n    </tbody>\n</table>\n</div>\n<p class=\"rc-scroll--x\">&nbsp;</p>\n</div>"
        },
        "Title": {
         "value": ""
        }
       }
      },
      {
       "uid": "21490587-9a14-47e7-b75c-8c82d8d20fbf",
       "componentName": "TitleAndParagraph",
       "dataSource": "/sitecore/content/Academy/Academy/Academy/Home/Veterinary/Canine atopic dermatitis and the owner/Data/Title and Paragraph 2",
       "restrictedPlaceHolder": null,
       "params": {
        "renderingId": "{C6E02A45-C0BD-4D75-BB75-601DC8099EB2}"
       },
       "fields": {
        "HeadingLevel": {
         "id": "b88aac1c-b17b-4af2-9278-aeaf6aa51696",
         "url": "/en/presentation/styles/heading-levels/h2",
         "name": "h2",
         "displayName": "h2",
         "fields": {
          "Value": {
           "value": "h2"
          }
         }
        },
        "Text": {
         "value": "<p><span style=\"font-size: 1.875rem; color: #e2001a;\">Outside the consultation&nbsp;</span></p>\n<p>\nAs noted above, a quality-of-life scale is an essential support tool when carrying out a follow-up [<strong title=\"Favrot C, Linek M, Mueller R, et al. Development of a questionnaire to assess the impact of atopic dermatitis on health-related quality of life of affected dogs and their owners. Vet Dermatol 2010;21(1):63-69.\" class=\"reference-snippet\">6</strong>][<strong title=\"Noli C, Minafo G, Galzerano M. Quality of life of dogs with skin diseases and their owners; Part 1: development and validation of a questionnaire. Vet Dermatol 2011;22(4):335-343.\" class=\"reference-snippet\">8</strong>] and this can be completed online, during a video consultation, or even via a smartphone app. This is in preference to other options such as lesion charts or pruritus scales, which the author finds unhelpful. Keeping in contact is also essential, and for serious cases group meetings with owners may be beneficial. &ldquo;Care contracts&rdquo; which assist in monitoring chronic diseases are suitable for dogs with CAD, and financial planning with a monthly breakdown of expenses will keep the owner engaged and facilitate early detection of complications or flare-ups, which can help avoid losing contact with the case.</p>\n<h2 class=\"rc-beta\"><span style=\"color: #e2001a;\">Conclusion</span></h2>\n<p id=\"first\">\n</p>\n<p>When dealing with CAD, longer consultations and over-reliance on external resources can be counterproductive, and it is much more effective if the veterinarian can show empathy for the owner&rsquo;s viewpoint; this must be coupled with a thorough understanding of canine atopic dermatitis and a willingness to offer appropriate treatment options, alongside judicious use of measures that help the owner comprehend the many ramifications of this complex disease.</p><span class='anchor-link' id='first'>&nbsp;</span>"
        },
        "Title": {
         "value": ""
        }
       }
      },
